{
 "slug": "pick-where-to-be-in-an-earthquake",
 "title": "Pick where to be when an earthquake hits: a doorway, outside, next to the couch, or under a table",
 "format": "pick-a-scenario",
 "root": "/home/claude/b3d/quake",
 "lines": [
  "Only one of these is the right move.",
  "Pick one: an earthquake hits. Where do you go?",
  "A doorway, outside, next to the couch, or under a table.",
  "Pick one, two, three or four, and comment. Quick.",
  "Number one, the doorway. It's the classic move, and it's a myth.",
  "It began with an old photo: a collapsed adobe house, with only its door frame standing.",
  "But in modern houses, doorways are no stronger than the rest.",
  "And they won't stop what hurts most people: falling and flying [objects](obb-jekts).",
  "Number two, run outside. Every instinct says go.",
  "But the floor is moving, and trying to run is how people fall.",
  "And outside, glass, bricks, and pieces of buildings can come raining down.",
  "Number three, curl up next to the couch. It's called the Triangle of Life.",
  "It went viral by email, but leading experts say its source is discredited.",
  "Falling TVs, lamps and bookcases are a far bigger danger than a collapse.",
  "Number four, under a sturdy table. This is the winner.",
  "Drop onto your hands and knees, and crawl under it.",
  "Cover your head and neck, and hold on. If it slides, move with it.",
  "No table? Get next to an inside wall, and cover your head.",
  "And if you're in bed, stay there, and cover your head with a pillow.",
  "Drop, cover, hold on. Millions practice it every October.",
  "So the next time the ground starts to shake, remember:"
 ],
